 "article": {
  "title": "Learning by Doing: The Return of the School Garden",
  "topic": "教育（劳动教育·校园菜园）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 301,
  "paras": [
   "For many children today, a tomato is something that comes wrapped in plastic at a supermarket. A growing number of schools are trying to change that by handing students a small piece of land and a packet of seeds. The school garden, once common in the countryside, is quietly returning to cities as well, and its supporters insist that it is not really about vegetables at all.",
   "A garden teaches science without a textbook. Students measure how fast a bean sprout grows, test which side of the yard gets more sun, and work out why some plants need far more water than others. They also meet the slow rhythm of nature: seeds do not care about deadlines. Waiting for the first shoot to appear can be the hardest lesson of all for a child used to instant screens.",
   "Teachers report other gains as well. A garden belongs to no single student, so watering and weeding have to be shared, and those who finish first learn to help the ones who lag behind. Children who rarely speak up in class often become the ones who notice a thirsty plant. Some schools carry the harvest into the kitchen; others sell the extra vegetables and put the money back into tools and seeds. In every case, the lesson is the same: effort and reward are connected.",
   "Yet a school garden is not easy to keep alive. It needs space, money and, above all, time — three things that crowded schools have in short supply. When examinations draw near, the garden is usually the first thing to be neglected. For that reason, experienced teachers argue that such projects must be built into the timetable rather than added on top of it. A garden that only survives in good weather, they warn, teaches exactly the wrong lesson."
  ],
  "zh": "对今天许多孩子来说，番茄是超市里用塑料膜包好的东西。越来越多学校正试图改变这一点：把一小块地和一包种子交到学生手里。曾经在乡村常见的校园菜园，如今也在城市里悄然回归——而支持者坚称，它讲的其实根本不是蔬菜。菜园是不用课本的科学课：学生测量豆苗长得多快、测试院子哪一侧日照更足、琢磨为什么有的植物需要多得多水；他们也体会到自然缓慢的节奏——种子不关心截止日期，等第一株嫩芽冒头，对习惯了即时屏幕的孩子而言可能是最难的一课。老师还看到另一种收获：菜园不属于任何一个学生，浇水和除草必须分担，先干完的人学着去帮落后的人；平时极少发言的孩子，往往成了最先发现哪株植物渴了的人。有的学校把收获带进厨房，有的把多余蔬菜卖掉，再把钱投回工具和种子——无论哪种，道理都一样：付出与回报是连着的。但菜园并不好养：它需要空间、钱，更需要时间，而这三样在拥挤的学校里样样紧缺。临近考试，菜园往往第一个被忽视。因此有经验的老师主张，这类项目必须写进课表，而不是加在课表之上；只在好天气里活下来的菜园，恰恰教了最错的一课。",
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "The school garden, once common in the countryside, is quietly returning to cities as well.",
    "cn": "曾经在乡村常见的校园菜园，如今也在城市里悄然回归。",
    "why": "逗号之间 once common in the countryside 是插入的形容词短语作非限制性定语，说明花园“过去的状态”；写作时可用 “X, once + 形容词/过去分词, is now …” 来对比今昔，位置灵活且能让句子有节奏。"
   },
   {
    "en": "Waiting for the first shoot to appear can be the hardest lesson of all for a child used to instant screens.",
    "cn": "等待第一株嫩芽冒出来，对习惯了即时屏幕的孩子来说，可能是最难的一课。",
    "why": "动名词短语 Waiting for … to appear 作主语，谓语用单数 can be；句尾 used to instant screens 是过去分词短语作后置定语（＝who is used to…），不要和 used to do“过去常常”混淆。"
   },
   {
    "en": "A garden that only survives in good weather, they warn, teaches exactly the wrong lesson.",
    "cn": "他们提醒说，只在好天气里活下来的菜园，恰恰教了最错的一课。",
    "why": "主干是 A garden … teaches …，中间的 they warn 是插入语（可视为 they warn that 的省略），把主语和谓语隔开。阅读长句时先跳过插入语找主干，再回看定语从句 that only survives in good weather。"
   }
  ],
